package Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.Shaders

import Vixen.Shaders.Core

// Doc 48 § 4.1's noise: one kernel with four bases — value · gradient · worley · white — under
// octaves, lacunarity, gain, tiling and a seed. Worley also answers with F1, F2 and a cell index,
// "which is what a splatter wants and what saves a flood fill downstream".
//
// ## ⚠ The basis is a uniform, and § 4.1 asked for a `[Permutation]`
//
// It cannot be one. `TexturePlanEvaluator.VariantFor` compiles `EffectKey.Of(kernel)` with no
// defines and caches the result on `(kernel, output format)` — there is no plumbing anywhere in the
// evaluator M1 built through which a plan could name a permutation value, so a `[Permutation]` here
// would silently take its `.rvn` default in every op for ever. That is the repository's registered
// trap (`PermutationKeys`) arriving from the other side: not a value missing from the key list, but
// no key list at all. A uniform is also what `Shaders/README.md` already argues for the blend mode,
// and the argument is stronger here — four bases times three output formats is twelve modules for a
// branch that every invocation in a dispatch takes the same way.
//
// ## ⚠ The hash is `Raven/Library/Core/Random.rvn`'s, called — and this file carried a copy of it
//
// `Random.Hash`, `Random.Combine` and `Random.ToFloat01` used to be written out here under a header
// saying they could not be reached, because "a texture-graph kernel is compiled alone —
// `RavenEffectCompiler.FromSources` is given one tree and no `.rvnlib`". Half of that was true and
// the half naming a cause was not. `FromSources` takes a *set* of texts and makes them one
// compilation; the evaluator was passing one text. `TextureKernelPrelude` passes the library beside
// every kernel now, so the import resolves and there is one hash rather than five — #635.
//
// ⚠ **`ComputeColor.ValueNoise` and `ComputeColor.FractalNoise` are still not called**, and that is
// a choice rather than a leftover: they are very nearly `ValueAt` and `Fractal` and *very nearly* is
// the problem. Neither takes a period, so neither can tile, which is what this kernel's `tiling`
// exists for. Widening the library's pair to carry a period is a change to the shader graph's noise
// and wants its own decision.
//
// ## The seed
//
// `seed` arrives as a float because `TexturePlanEvaluator` supplies it that way — twenty-four bits of
// `TexturePlan.SeedFor(op)`, hashed on the CPU where it is the same on every machine. What is needed
// of it here is only that two noise ops disagree, and twenty-four bits of a mixed seed is that.
//
// ## Tiling, and what it needs of the other parameters
//
// ⚠ **A tiling noise is a lattice with an integer period, so tiling is exact only when `scale` is a
// whole number of cells and `lacunarity` is a whole number too.** With `scale = 5.5` there is no
// integer period the lattice can wrap at and the seam reappears; the kernel rounds rather than
// refuses, because a bake is not the place to throw. The node in M4 is where a non-integer scale
// under tiling is worth a diagnostic.
//
// ## Resolution
//
// Everything here is a function of the normalised position, so a noise field is the same picture at
// every resolution — which is also why **white noise is per *cell* and not per texel.** A per-texel
// white noise is a different image at 1K and at 4K by construction, and doc 48 § D8 requires that it
// not be. An author who wants one texel per sample sets `scale` to the resolution and says so.

shader Noise {
    /// 0 value · 1 gradient · 2 worley · 3 white.
    var basis: int = 0

    /// How many cells across the image at the first octave.
    var scale: float = 8f

    /// How many octaves are summed. Ignored by worley — see `Worley`.
    var octaves: int = 1

    /// The frequency multiplier between octaves. 2 is the usual choice and the only one that tiles.
    var lacunarity: float = 2f

    /// The amplitude multiplier between octaves. 0.5 is the usual choice.
    var gain: float = 0.5f

    /// Non-zero to wrap the lattice, so the image is seamless when repeated.
    var tiling: int = 0

    /// The op's own seed, already mixed. See `TexturePlan.SeedFor`.
    var seed: float = 0f

    [Format("rgba16f")] var target: RWTexture2D<float4>

    const val TwoPi: float = 6.2831853f

    /// 2D Perlin peaks at √2 / 2, so this is what maps its range onto 0..1.
    const val PerlinScale: float = 0.70710678f

    /// The most octaves a plan can ask for. A ceiling rather than a runtime bound, for `Blur`'s
    /// reason: an octave count arriving as a NaN would be a loop no invocation leaves.
    const val MaxOctaves: int = 8

    /// The furthest a worley feature point can be, in cells, given a 3×3 search.
    const val WorleyFar: float = 4f

    /// The seed as the integer the hash wants.
    func Seeded(): uint => uint(int(max(seed, 0f)))

    /// One lattice coordinate, wrapped when the noise tiles.
    func Fold(value: int, period: int): int {
        if (tiling == 0) {
            return value
        }

        if (period <= 0) {
            return value
        }

        // Twice, because `%` follows the sign of the dividend in both of Raven's backends and a cell
        // left of the origin would otherwise fold to a negative index — which hashes perfectly well
        // and is a different cell from the one on the far side, so the seam comes back.
        return ((value % period) + period) % period
    }

    /// The hash of one cell, under the seed and the tiling period.
    ///
    /// ⚠ A block body rather than a `=>` broken over two lines. A newline ends a statement in Raven,
    /// so the continuation would have been a second statement and the first would have had no
    /// expression at all — which `RVN1001` does catch here, and does not catch when the continuation
    /// begins with an operator.
    func CellHash(x: int, y: int, period: int): uint {
        return Random.Combine(Random.Combine(uint(Fold(x, period)), uint(Fold(y, period))), Seeded())
    }

    /// Smoothed value noise: a hash per lattice corner, bilinear under a smoothstep.
    func ValueAt(position: float2, period: int): float {
        val cell = floor(position)
        val local = position - cell
        val weights = local * local * (3f - 2f * local)
        val x = int(cell.x)
        val y = int(cell.y)

        val c00 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x, y, period))
        val c10 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x + 1, y, period))
        val c01 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x, y + 1, period))
        val c11 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x + 1, y + 1, period))

        return lerp(lerp(c00, c10, weights.x), lerp(c01, c11, weights.x), weights.y)
    }

    /// One corner's gradient dotted with the offset to it.
    func GradientDot(x: int, y: int, period: int, delta: float2): float {
        val angle = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x, y, period)) * TwoPi

        return delta.x * cos(angle) + delta.y * sin(angle)
    }

    /// Gradient (Perlin) noise, remapped from ±√2/2 onto 0..1.
    func GradientAt(position: float2, period: int): float {
        val cell = floor(position)
        val local = position - cell
        val fade = local * local * local * (local * (local * 6f - 15f) + 10f)
        val x = int(cell.x)
        val y = int(cell.y)

        val g00 = GradientDot(x, y, period, local)
        val g10 = GradientDot(x + 1, y, period, local - float2(1f, 0f))
        val g01 = GradientDot(x, y + 1, period, local - float2(0f, 1f))
        val g11 = GradientDot(x + 1, y + 1, period, local - float2(1f, 1f))

        val under = lerp(g00, g10, fade.x)
        val over = lerp(g01, g11, fade.x)

        return lerp(under, over, fade.y) * PerlinScale + 0.5f
    }

    /// One uncorrelated value per cell — value noise without the interpolation.
    func WhiteAt(position: float2, period: int): float {
        val cell = floor(position)

        return Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(int(cell.x), int(cell.y), period))
    }

    /// Whichever basis a fractal octave is made of.
    func Octave(position: float2, period: int): float {
        if (basis == 1) {
            return GradientAt(position, period)
        }

        if (basis == 3) {
            return WhiteAt(position, period)
        }

        return ValueAt(position, period)
    }

    /// Octaves at multiplying frequency and multiplying amplitude, normalised back to 0..1.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Normalised by the sum of the amplitudes rather than by a constant**, so a gain that is not
    /// 0.5 still fills the range — an octave stack divided by two per level is a field that never
    /// reaches white and reads as a washed-out noise nobody can find the cause of.
    func Fractal(uv: float2): float {
        var total = 0f
        var amplitude = 1f
        var normalisation = 0f
        var frequency = 1f
        val wanted = clamp(octaves, 1, MaxOctaves)

        for (octave in 0 .. MaxOctaves - 1) {
            if (octave >= wanted) {
                break
            }

            val period = int(round(max(scale, 1f) * frequency))
            val stride = tiling == 0 ? max(scale, 0.00001f) * frequency : float(max(period, 1))

            total = total + Octave(uv * stride, period) * amplitude
            normalisation = normalisation + amplitude
            frequency = frequency * max(lacunarity, 1f)
            amplitude = amplitude * max(gain, 0f)
        }

        return total / max(normalisation, 0.000001f)
    }

    /// F1, F2 and a cell index, from one lattice.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Worley ignores `octaves`, deliberately.** F1 summed over octaves is a plausible grey field,
    /// but the F2 and the cell index of a sum of lattices name nothing — and the cell index is the
    /// whole reason § 4.1 gives this basis its own two extra channels. A graph that wants fractal
    /// worley sums two ops with a `Blend`, where the thing being summed is visible.
    func Worley(uv: float2): float3 {
        val period = int(round(max(scale, 1f)))
        val stride = tiling == 0 ? max(scale, 0.00001f) : float(max(period, 1))
        val position = uv * stride
        val origin = floor(position)

        var f1 = WorleyFar
        var f2 = WorleyFar
        var index = 0f

        for (row in -1 .. 1) {
            for (column in -1 .. 1) {
                val x = int(origin.x) + column
                val y = int(origin.y) + row
                val hash = CellHash(x, y, period)
                val jitter = float2(Random.ToFloat01(hash), Random.ToFloat01(Random.Hash(hash ^ 0x68BC21EBu)))
                val point = float2(origin.x + float(column), origin.y + float(row)) + jitter
                val reach = length(point - position)

                if (reach < f1) {
                    f2 = f1
                    f1 = reach
                    index = Random.ToFloat01(Random.Hash(hash ^ 0x1B873593u))
                } else {
                    if (reach < f2) {
                        f2 = reach
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // ⚠ **Reported in units of two cells, so that neither distance clips.** A 3×3 search's
        // second-nearest point is regularly further than one cell away, and a `saturate` at one cell
        // would flatten exactly the channel a splatter uses to find a cell's border — F2 would read
        // as a wide plateau of white and the difference F2 − F1, which is the border, would vanish
        // along with it. Halving both keeps the pair comparable and leaves headroom.
        return float3(saturate(f1 * 0.5f), saturate(f2 * 0.5f), index)
    }

    [ComputeShader(8, 8, 1)]
    func Main([Semantic("SV_DispatchThreadID")] id: uint3) {
        val coord = int2(int(id.x), int(id.y))
        val size = target.GetDimensions()

        if (coord.x >= size.x || coord.y >= size.y) {
            return
        }

        val uv = float2((float(coord.x) + 0.5f) / float(size.x), (float(coord.y) + 0.5f) / float(size.y))

        // ⚠ Worley is the one basis whose output is not grey, and the channels are named here rather
        // than left to a reader: red is F1, green is F2, blue is the cell index. Doc 48 § 4.1 asks for
        // "grey + cell id" and this is it — a downstream `Channel Shuffle` is what picks one out.
        if (basis == 2) {
            val cells = Worley(uv)

            target.Store(coord, float4(cells.x, cells.y, cells.z, 1f))

            return
        }

        val value = saturate(Fractal(uv))

        target.Store(coord, float4(value, value, value, 1f))
    }
}
